<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="sso"
      updated="July 31, 2026"
      source_path="portal/apps/emisar_web/lib/emisar_web/controllers/marketing_html/docs_sso.html.heex"
      toc={[
        {"sso-login", "Setting up SSO login"},
        {"operations", "Common provider operations"},
        {"providers", "Provider guides"},
        {"generic-oidc", "Any other OIDC provider"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="sso">
        <:dek>
          On Team and Enterprise plans, your team signs in to emisar with your own identity
          provider — Google Workspace, Okta, Microsoft Entra, JumpCloud, Keycloak, or any
          compliant OIDC.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This page explains how SSO works in emisar and sets up the connection. Provisioning and
        deprovisioning from the directory is a separate piece that builds on it: <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >directory sync (SCIM)</.link>.
        Both are managed from <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on</strong>
        in your emisar workspace, and the
        <a href="#providers" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">provider guides</a>
        below walk each console step by step.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso-login">Setting up SSO login</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two things have to exist: an OAuth application at your identity provider, and a connection
        in emisar pointing at it. You need an emisar owner or admin, plus enough access at the
        provider to create an app. Open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/sso/new"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</a>
        and choose your provider type; the form then names the exact admin-console path for it, and
        the <a href="#providers" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">provider guides</a>
        below walk every screen with screenshots. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        At the provider, create a
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">confidential web application</strong>
        — one with a client secret. A public or single-page client will not work, because emisar
        authenticates with that secret. Give the app emisar's redirect URI, which the form shows
        with a copy button:
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>
        on your emisar host. emisar always sends that exact URI and never takes it from the
        request, so paste it verbatim. If the provider offers <strong class="text-zinc-200">DPoP</strong>, leave it off — emisar reads the ID token and
        never calls an API with the access token, so sender-constrained tokens buy nothing here and
        break the token request.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Then come back to emisar and fill in the connection. It has four sections, and the rest of
        this section explains them.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="connection-fields">Provider and OIDC connection</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">provider type</strong>
        selects the preset — it decides which claim emisar can bind identities to and which
        provider-specific quirks it applies, so pick the real one rather than Generic OIDC. The
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">display name</strong>
        is what your members see on the sign-in page, so name it the way they would say it out
        loud: "Acme Okta".
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">issuer URL</strong>
        is the one endpoint you have to get right. emisar fetches the OIDC discovery document from
        it and derives every other endpoint, which is why you never paste authorization, token, or
        JWKS URLs by hand. It must be HTTPS and reachable from emisar. Each provider has its own
        shape — your Okta org URL, your Keycloak realm URL, your Entra tenant — and the form shows
        the right one once you pick a type.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">client ID</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">client secret</strong>
        come from the app you just created. The secret is write-only: it is stored server-side,
        redacted in the interface, and never rendered back to you. It cannot be hashed the way a
        password is, because emisar has to replay it to your provider on every sign-in; protecting
        it at rest is an infrastructure concern. When you edit the connection later, leaving the
        field blank keeps the secret you already have.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>
        is what an identity is permanently bound to. emisar offers only the claim your provider
        actually issues — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        everywhere, and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
        for Microsoft Entra — so there is nothing to decide here beyond leaving it alone. It
        matters more than it looks: it is what
        <a href="#subject-not-email" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          identities are matched on
        </a>
        instead of email, and it is the value your directory's
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        ><code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code></.link>
        must carry for sign-in and directory sync to converge on one person.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="provisioning-fields">Who gets in, and what they get</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">New-user provisioning</strong>
        decides what happens the first time someone signs in through this connection who has no
        emisar member yet. <span class="text-zinc-300">Auto-provision</span>
        creates them on the spot; <span class="text-zinc-300">Manual</span>
        holds the sign-in as a pending request for an admin to approve. Auto-provision suits a
        connection whose provider-side app is already assigned to exactly the right people, which
        is the usual arrangement; manual suits a provider you do not control that tightly.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Turning on
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          directory sync
        </.link>
        overrides this. The directory then decides who exists, so a sign-in it never provisioned is
        held for approval whichever setting you picked — an account the directory does not know
        about cannot walk in through the login page.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default role for new users</strong>
        is where a newly provisioned member lands: viewer, operator, billing manager, or admin.
        Provisioning never grants <span class="text-zinc-300">owner</span>
        — that stays a deliberate human grant, so no provider misconfiguration can mint one. If you
        map directory groups to roles, the mapping wins and this is only the fallback for someone
        in no mapped group.
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Roles</.link>
        explains what each one can do.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default runner access</strong>
        is the other half of that landing: which hosts the new member can even see and dispatch to.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">No runners</span>
        is the safe default and the one we would pick — they arrive able to read the console and
        nothing else, and you widen it deliberately.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">All runners</span>
        includes every runner you enroll in the future, not just today's fleet.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">Selected runners</span>
        pins them to named runner groups or hosts. Role and runner access are independent: an
        operator with no runners can dispatch nothing, which is exactly what you want for someone
        who has not been given a fleet yet.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Allowed email domain</strong>
        is optional. It refuses a sign-in whose provider-verified address is on another domain.
        Leave it blank to accept any address the provider returns. Either way an address your
        provider does not mark as verified is never trusted.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="activation-fields">Security and activation</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        One checkbox says <em>sign-in through this provider satisfies the account's 2FA
        requirement</em>. Turn it on only if the provider enforces MFA itself — then emisar accepts
        the provider's challenge instead of asking for a second factor of its own. Turn it on for a
        provider that does not, and every member signing in through it walks past your 2FA
        requirement. The
        <a href="#sso-mfa" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">section below</a>
        covers how that interacts with the rest of the account.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Before saving, press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>. emisar runs a
        real OIDC discovery round-trip against the issuer and shows the authorization, token, and
        JWKS endpoints it found, so a typo or an unreachable provider surfaces here rather than at
        your team's first sign-in. Then tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>
        and save — until you do, the connection exists but nobody can sign in through it, which is
        also how you park one without deleting it.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="signing-in">How members sign in</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the sign-in page, members click <strong class="text-zinc-200">"Continue with single sign-on"</strong>, which sends them to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso</code>; there they enter their team's address to reach that team's sign-in page, pick your provider, authenticate at the IdP, and come back signed in. An <strong class="text-zinc-200">allowed email domain</strong>, if set, is a guardrail on the return trip: a login whose IdP-verified domain doesn't match is refused.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="subject-not-email">
        Identities are matched by the IdP's subject, never by email
      </.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar binds an SSO login to the provider plus the IdP's stable subject identifier (the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        claim, or whichever <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>
        your provider uses) — <strong class="text-zinc-200">never by email address</strong>.
        Email-matching an SSO login onto an existing account is a known account-takeover vector, so
        we don't do it. The practical consequence: a just-in-time provisioned SSO user is a
        distinct principal, not a merge into a pre-existing account that happens to share
        an email. If an SSO email collides with an existing user, sign-in is refused rather than
        silently linked — an admin links the identity deliberately.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">First login provisions the user</strong>
        at the connection's default role (with the directory as the email authority).
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="sso-mfa">SSO sessions and your MFA requirement</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each connection has a per-provider toggle — <em>"sign-in through this provider satisfies
        the account's 2FA requirement."</em>
        When it's on, an SSO session satisfies an account that
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          requires MFA
        </.link>
        without a second emisar TOTP prompt — your IdP enforces the MFA. Turn it off if you'd
        rather emisar still challenge. Magic-link sign-in is unaffected and is challenged for 2FA
        as usual.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="operations">Common provider operations</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="require-sso">Require SSO for the account</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        First complete a real sign-in through the connection in another browser. The form's
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>
        checks discovery and endpoint reachability, but it cannot prove the IdP assigned your user,
        accepted the callback URI, or returned the required claims. Keep an owner session open
        while you test. Then open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Team → Security</a>
        and turn on <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require single sign-on</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Once required, a magic-link session cannot enter this account: emisar sends the member
        through one of this account's enabled providers on their next navigation. The requirement
        cannot be enabled with no active connection, and emisar refuses to disable or delete the
        last active connection while the requirement remains on. Turn off Require SSO before
        retiring that last connection.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="rotate-secret">Rotate a client secret</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Create the replacement secret at your IdP, paste it into the connection, and save. The
        secret is write-only, so leaving the field blank keeps the existing value. Do not delete
        the old IdP secret until a new sign-in succeeds through the saved connection.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="move-provider">Move to another issuer or client</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        After a connection has signed someone in, its issuer, client ID, and identifier claim are
        fixed. Those fields define what a stored subject means; repointing them could make an
        existing identity refer to someone else. To move tenants, issuers, or clients, add a new
        connection and migrate assignments to it. Client-secret rotation stays available on the
        original connection.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="disable-provider">Disable or delete a connection</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Disabling stops new sign-ins but keeps the configuration so you can enable it again.
        Deleting retires the connection and dismisses any manual-approval requests still waiting on
        it. Both actions revoke active sessions authenticated through identities on that connection;
        they do not delete the users or their audit history.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An account can have several provider types, but only one enabled connection of each type.
        An allowed email domain can belong to only one enabled emisar connection, so a duplicate
        domain must be removed or disabled before another connection can claim it.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="providers">Provider guides</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each guide walks one provider end to end — sign-in first, then directory sync — with
        screenshots of the console screens along the way.
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li id="okta" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/okta"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Okta</.link>
            — an OIDC web app for sign-in and a separate SCIM app for the directory.
            Verified against a live Okta Integrator org on July 27, 2026.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="entra" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Microsoft Entra</.link>
            — an app registration with the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
            claim, and an enterprise application for provisioning.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="jumpcloud" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/jumpcloud"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >JumpCloud</.link>
            — one custom application carries sign-in and provisioning together.
            Verified against a live JumpCloud tenant on July 31, 2026.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="keycloak" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/keycloak"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Keycloak</.link>
            — a confidential client with PKCE; no outbound SCIM.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="google-workspace" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/google-workspace"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Google Workspace</.link>
            — an Internal OAuth client and a locked, prefilled issuer; no outbound SCIM.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="generic-oidc">Any other OIDC provider</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The named providers above are presets over one standard flow, not separate integrations.
        A provider that meets the contract below works through
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Generic OpenID Connect</strong>
        — including Auth0, Ping, OneLogin, Authentik, or an in-house IdP. This connection is OIDC
        authorization code only; it does not accept SAML.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need three things from your provider, and emisar derives the rest:
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A confidential web client</strong>
            with a client secret, holding emisar's callback as a redirect URI. Public or SPA
            clients will not do — emisar authenticates with the secret.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The issuer URL</strong>
            — whatever serves <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>. emisar fetches discovery from there,
            so you never paste individual endpoints, and it must be HTTPS and reachable from
            emisar.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The client ID and secret.</strong>
            emisar accepts either
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">client_secret_post</code>
            or <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">client_secret_basic</code>, whichever your provider advertises.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="oidc-contract">OIDC contract</.docs_h3>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Part</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What emisar requires</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Discovery</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                An HTTPS issuer with a valid OpenID Provider Configuration. Its authorization,
                token, and JWKS endpoints must also be public HTTPS URLs; private, loopback, and
                cloud-metadata targets are refused.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Authorization</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Authorization code flow with PKCE. emisar uses S256 when the provider advertises
                it and accepts plain only when that is the provider's advertised PKCE method.
                emisar requests <code>openid</code>, <code>email</code>, and <code>profile</code>, plus
                <code>state</code>
                and <code>nonce</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Client authentication</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                A confidential client using <code>client_secret_post</code>
                or <code>client_secret_basic</code>. Public clients and signing-key client
                authentication are not supported.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">ID-token claims</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                A stable <code>sub</code>, an <code>email</code>
                marked by <code>email_verified: true</code>, and optionally <code>name</code>. emisar reads
                these from the ID token; it does not call UserInfo. The Entra preset uses
                <code>oid</code>
                instead of <code>sub</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Validation</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The callback must pass state, nonce, PKCE, ID-token signature, exact issuer,
                audience, and expiration checks before emisar resolves an identity.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two settings to leave alone unless your provider forces otherwise: keep
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">DPoP</strong>
        off — emisar reads the ID token only and never calls an API with the access token, so
        sender-constrained tokens add nothing and break the token request — and keep the identifier
        claim on <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>. The one provider that needs
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
        instead is <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Microsoft Entra</.link>, for the reason
        that guide explains.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>
        on the form runs a real discovery round-trip and shows the authorization, token, and JWKS
        endpoints it found, so a wrong issuer fails before you save rather than at someone's first
        sign-in.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Symptom</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Test connection cannot load discovery
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the issuer, not an authorization or token URL. Confirm it serves
                <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>
                over public HTTPS and does not
                publish private or non-HTTPS endpoints.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                The IdP rejects the callback or token exchange
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Compare the redirect URI byte for byte, confirm the app is a confidential web
                client, and replace an expired client secret. For a generic provider, confirm a
                PKCE method and one of the two supported secret-auth methods are advertised.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Authentication succeeds but emisar refuses the identity
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Inspect the ID token: it must contain the configured stable identifier and a
                verified email. UserInfo-only email or profile claims are not read. If an existing
                emisar user has that email, an admin must link the provider identity deliberately;
                emisar never merges by email.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Members loop back to SSO after Require SSO
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the owner session you kept open to turn off Require SSO, then complete a real
                provider sign-in before enabling it again. If no owner session remains, contact <a
                  href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.support()}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >
                  support
                </a>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                SSO and SCIM create different identities
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Make SCIM <code>externalId</code>
                equal the OIDC identifier claim. Entra needs <code>objectId</code>
                on the SCIM side and <code>oid</code>
                on the OIDC side.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every SSO sign-in lands in the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >audit log</.link>
        with the authentication method it used, so "who did this, and how did they get in" is one
        query rather than a correlation exercise across two systems.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
